\title{Spartan Protocol and Pancake Bunny Hack (2021)}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2025-10-09}

\p{
    More examples of successful flash loan attacks include
    attacks on the Spartan Protocol and Pancake Bunny, two DeFi contracts on the Binance Smart Chain
    (BSC). Attackers used flash loans to make huge trades and temporarily manipulate market prices of
    certain assets. Both of these contracts used these market prices in the contract logic, and in both cases
    this lead to pathological—though, again, correct according to the flawed specification—contract behavior.
    In May 2021, an attacker [drained](https://bscscan.com/tx/0xb64ae25b0d836c25d115a9368319902c972a0215bd108ae17b1b9617dfb93af8) the Spartan Protocol contract for a profit of roughly the [equivalent of 30 million USD](https://peckshield-94632.medium.com/the-spartan-incident-root-cause-analysis-b14135d3415f). Another [attacker](https://bscscan.com/address/0x158c244b62058330f2c328c720b072d8db2c612f) [drained](https://x.com/PancakeBunnyFin/status/1395173389208334342) Pancake Bunny of 114k WBNB and 697k BUNNY tokens, amounting to about [45 million USD at the time in lost funds](https://cointelegraph.com/news/pancakebunny-tanks-96-following-200m-flash-loan-exploit).
}